# Design sketch: controlled Host startup waits for real owner registration

Status: design only, pending the default three-schedule actual RED characterization. This is not an applied ADR, production patch, startup fix, or qualification record. The unchanged fixture and wrapper establish separate schedule observations; they do not by themselves prove the cause of every prior W2 document failure.

## Present ordering

Host mount creates a hidden input HWND and SDK broker identity, then invoke_lifecycle waits with future.get() without pumping the owner queue. mount_on_engine creates and binds the controlled endpoint (which queues nonterminal OLE_REGISTER), installs callbacks/bindings, requests the first local navigation with wkeLoadFileW, and resizes before returning. The child is shown only afterward. Normal later owner WM_SIZE may advance SDK geometry before the original registration TIMERPROC runs. With an old receipt, strict SDK enter fails; if vendor ACK arrives first, the nonterminal queued registration is instead cancelled by its old tuple/epoch. A same-size WM_SIZE filter would not remove navigation/context generation changes or establish that RegisterDragDrop ran.

The current SDK onNativeComplete posts a wake but has no registration-specific completion ledger. Total task completion is insufficient: updateIdentity also posts a genuine cancellation continuation. inspectDrain is engine-owner-only and nonblocking; it must never be called from onNativeEnter/Leave/Complete.

## Proposed two-stage mount, preserving fail-closed admission

1. Keep the public start lifecycle guard active, the child hidden, interaction routing inactive, and main-context receipt unaccepted. Leave bridge_accepting false until startup prerequisites complete. Do not set cursor_broker's document_request_pending merely to represent this startup phase: that existing flag rejects nonterminal native entry, including the registration we need to observe.
2. On the engine owner, prime the controlled endpoint: validate required APIs, create/bind using the initial full tuple, retain all callback/HWND/DragHandle ownership, and establish the required interaction endpoint. Return from that engine task before waiting on the UI owner. Defer user script-context/document callbacks, JS/bridge binding activation and the first page request until the fence. Delaying callbacks is not a license to ignore any actual vendor document commit; unexpected automatic generation advancement during prime must remain a failure and needs a real Host regression.
3. On the actual HWND owner, dispatch only the genuine broker registration timer for this retained child. Keep the existing enter checks, tuple/epoch cancellation and original TIMERPROC/body untouched. If a private SDK completion ledger is needed, copy only fixed receipt fields for kind OLE_REGISTER plus its actual cancelled flag under the existing mutex; it allocates nothing, calls no WKE and cannot grant admission. Require exactly one noncancelled registration completion for the bound endpoint/current initial tuple. A completion callback alone is not registration success.
4. After the owner callback has returned and no SDK native scope remains, invoke a fresh short engine task to inspectDrain. Require a validated matching endpoint/struct, status OK, closing 0, queued/running/nativeScopes/engineCallbacks all 0 and oleRegistered 1. The engine is not parked waiting for the owner: it remains free to service its FIFO/native queue and the query runs on its real owner. SDK and vendor snapshots remain separately observed, not atomic. Only then install/enable deferred callbacks and bridge bindings, issue the first navigation on that same engine owner, and allow the existing document/context ACK rules to arm readiness. Activate routing/show only after this stage returns successfully.
5. Any cancelled registration, failed admission, vendor FAILED, absent registration, invalid receipt, owner loss or true deadline is a startup failure. Do not reload, replay OLE_REGISTER, make it terminal, grant a pending receipt, fabricate ACK or skip resize. Normal subsequent geometry/document updates retain their present strict checks.

## Owner dispatch and lifetime boundary requiring implementation review

A fence must not reuse close_cursor_endpoint's all-message child pump or invoke_lifecycle's blocking wait as a general UI wait. The current broker timer identifier is private vendor constant 0x15324547; matching HWND/message/id and the actual loaded module's TIMERPROC is a possible narrow dispatch invariant, not a newly public authorization API. Its cross-module contract must be explicit and reviewed against the supported controlled DLL. Unknown queued timers/messages must not be dispatched or lost to make progress; no user/JS/business callout may be introduced by the fence. Windows sent-message reentrancy and the child WndProc's owner forwarding need a narrow review as well; the public lifecycle guard alone does not prove all internal dispatch paths are harmless.

A failed or timed-out fence does not cancel a native callback or permit C++ owner unwind. Retain the HWND, broker callback storage, vendor endpoint and DragHandle until actual terminal revoke, real drain/destroy receipt and SDK finish_close. Existing adapter_cleanup_pending ownership survives failed start. If safe cleanup cannot complete, the application must retain the Host for a real later cleanup attempt or fail-stop under the external Owned Job; it must not delete borrowed owners, detach the engine, clear a registry or FreeLibrary an initialized module. No new timeout means natural completion or business rollback.

## Minimal follow-up regressions after actual RED, before adoption

Keep the current default fixture unchanged as a historical source-specific 0/2/2 characterization. Add a fresh normal-SDK Host startup regression which proves real registration before the first intended page request, and observes actual vendorFailed/oleRegistered fields before success. Exercise UI-delayed and engine-ACK-first scheduling, real geometry-changing WM_SIZE, and an unexpected prime context/identity transition; do not infer these observations from rendered DOM. Ensure initial registration is not silently cancelled and one successful registration is balanced by terminal owner revoke/drain/destroy. Include actual registration failure/deadline with retained-owner cleanup, reentrant public start/resize/close rejection during the fence, and a second Host on the shared runtime (no global engine-pump suspension). Every engine hold/event in a test needs unconditional release or external fail-stop before owner cleanup, and every accepted success must bind actual PID/decimal-string FILETIME, DLL/source bytes and natural whole-Job closure.

Implementation is expected to be a private Host split plus, only if necessary, a fixed private broker completion snapshot. No vendor algorithm or public ABI change is planned by this sketch. A cancelled original task cannot be safely re-admitted by weakening SDK enter; if actual priming itself advances vendor identity before registration, stop and reassess the real ordering instead of claiming the two-stage split is sufficient.

